(_c1.chapters = _c1.chapters || []).push(
  { n:1, summary:"First Chronicles opens with a sweeping set of genealogies that trace the covenant people back to Adam. The line runs from Adam through Noah's sons to Abraham, then follows Abraham's sons Isaac and Ishmael, Isaac's sons Esau and Israel, and settles on Judah's family. Along the way it notes the nations that came from Japheth, Ham, and Shem, the peoples descended from Ishmael and from Keturah, and the clans of Edom from Esau and Seir. One marker stands out: Cush begat Nimrod, who began to be mighty upon the earth, and Peleg was born in the days when the earth was divided. The chapter closes by listing the kings and dukes who ruled Edom before any king reigned over Israel. Read as an introduction, these lists declare who Israel is and where every family fits in the Lord's story before the book turns to David.",
    context:"An anonymous post-exilic compiler opens the book with genealogies from Adam to the tribes, anchoring the identity of the covenant people.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"1 Chronicles 1:19", text:"And unto Eber were born two sons: the name of the one was Peleg; because in his days the earth was divided: and his brother's name was Joktan." },
      { ref:"1 Chronicles 1:34", text:"And Abraham begat Isaac. The sons of Isaac; Esau and Israel." },
      { ref:"1 Chronicles 1:43", text:"Now these are the kings that reigned in the land of Edom before any king reigned over the children of Israel; Bela the son of Beor: and the name of his city was Dinhabah." }
    ],
    takeaway:"Knowing where you come from in God's covenant story anchors who you are and why it matters.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"Whose name is given as another name for Abram?", choices:["Abraham","Israel","Isaac","Joktan"], a:1, why:"Verse 27 reads, Abram; the same is Abraham." },
      { q:"What does this chapter record about the kings of Edom?", choices:["They reigned in Edom before any king reigned over the children of Israel","They were all driven out by Moses","They paid yearly tribute to Solomon","They descended from Ishmael"], a:2, why:"Verse 43 introduces the kings that reigned in the land of Edom before any king reigned over the children of Israel, beginning with Bela the son of Beor." },
      { q:"From which three sons of Noah does the chapter trace the nations?", choices:["Cain, Abel, and Seth","Shem, Ham, and Japheth","Isaac, Ishmael, and Esau","Nebaioth, Kedar, and Kedemah"], a:3, why:"Verse 4 lists Noah's sons Shem, Ham, and Japheth, and the families of the nations follow from each of them." }
    ] },
  { n:2, summary:"Chapter two narrows the record to Israel's twelve sons, then follows Judah, the royal tribe. Judah's sons through Tamar were Pharez and Zerah, and the line moves through Pharez to Hezron and Ram, down through Nahshon, Boaz, Obed, and Jesse. Jesse's seven sons end with David the seventh, and his sisters were Zeruiah and Abigail, mothers of Joab, Abishai, Asahel, and Amasa. The chapter also maps side branches: Achar the troubler of Israel appears in Zerah's family, Jerahmeel's descendants fill a long section, and Caleb's line names fathers of towns like Beth-zur and Kirjath-jearim, with Salma tied to Bethlehem and the families of the scribes at Jabez noted as Kenites. The genealogy deliberately places David's family at the heart of Judah's story, preparing the book's focus on his kingdom.",
    context:"The genealogy of Judah runs from the tribal fathers through Boaz to David, rooting the coming king in Israel's covenant history.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"1 Chronicles 2:1", text:"These are the sons of Israel; Reuben, Simeon, Levi, and Judah, Issachar, and Zebulun," },
      { ref:"1 Chronicles 2:7", text:"And the sons of Carmi; Achar, the troubler of Israel, who transgressed in the thing accursed." },
      { ref:"1 Chronicles 2:16", text:"Whose sisters were Zeruiah, and Abigail. And the sons of Zeruiah; Abishai, and Joab, and Asahel, three." }
    ],
    takeaway:"The Lord prepares His purposes through generations of ordinary, imperfect families.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"Who was David's father, named in Judah's genealogy?", choices:["Jesse","Boaz","Nahshon","Hezron"], a:0, why:"Obed begat Jesse, and Jesse's sons ran down to David the seventh." },
      { q:"Which descendant in Zerah's family is called the troubler of Israel?", choices:["Carmi","Zimri","Achar","Ethan"], a:2, why:"Verse 7 names Achar, the troubler of Israel, who transgressed in the thing accursed." },
      { q:"Which of Jesse's sons was David?", choices:["The firstborn","The third","The fifth","The seventh"], a:3, why:"The list runs from Eliab the firstborn to Ozem the sixth, and David the seventh." }
    ] },
  { n:3, summary:"The record now lists David's own sons. Six were born to him in Hebron, including Amnon, Daniel, Absalom, and Adonijah, during the seven and a half years he reigned there; others, including Shimea, Shobab, Nathan, and Solomon, were born in Jerusalem, where he reigned thirty-three years. Tamar is named their sister. The line then runs from Solomon through the kings of Judah — Rehoboam, Abia, Asa, Jehoshaphat, and onward to Hezekiah, Manasseh, and Josiah. It follows Josiah's sons to Jehoiakim and Jeconiah, whose line continues through the exile with Zerubbabel, Hananiah, and several generations more. In this way the chapter anchors both the throne of David and the family line that endured past the Babylonian captivity, ending with the sons of Elioenai.",
    context:"David's own family line is traced from Hebron and Jerusalem through Judah's kings, the Babylonian exile, and the generations that followed.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"1 Chronicles 3:4", text:"These six were born unto him in Hebron; and there he reigned seven years and six months: and in Jerusalem he reigned thirty and three years." },
      { ref:"1 Chronicles 3:5", text:"And these were born unto him in Jerusalem; Shimea, and Shobab, and Nathan, and Solomon, four, of Bath-shua the daughter of Ammiel:" },
      { ref:"1 Chronicles 3:19", text:"And the sons of Pedaiah were, Zerubbabel, and Shimei: and the sons of Zerubbabel; Meshullam, and Hananiah, and Shelomith their sister:" }
    ],
    takeaway:"God keeps His covenant promises through every season — even exile — until they are fulfilled.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"How many of David's sons were born to him in Hebron?", choices:["Three","Six","Nine","Twelve"], a:0, why:"Verses 1-4 name six sons born in Hebron, where David reigned seven years and six months." },
      { q:"Which of David's sons, born in Jerusalem, continued the royal line?", choices:["Absalom","Solomon","Amnon","Adonijah"], a:1, why:"Solomon was born in Jerusalem, and Solomon's son was Rehoboam, beginning the list of Judah's kings." },
      { q:"Which man appears in the Davidic line after Jeconiah, carrying the family past the captivity?", choices:["Hezekiah","Josiah","Rehoboam","Zerubbabel"], a:3, why:"Jeconiah's descendants included Pedaiah, whose sons were Zerubbabel and Shimei, and Zerubbabel's family carries the record onward." }
    ] },
  { n:4, summary:"Judah's family continues through Hur and other branches, naming fathers of towns such as Bethlehem's clan and Tekoa, and families of craftsmen and potters. The best-known moment belongs to Jabez, who was more honourable than his brethren and named for the sorrow he caused his mother. Jabez called on the God of Israel, asking Him to bless him indeed, enlarge his coast, let His hand be with him, and keep him from evil, so that it might not grieve him; the record says simply that God granted him that which he requested. The chapter then turns to Simeon's descendants, their cities and villages, their princes, and how some of them found fat and quiet pasture, destroyed the former inhabitants in Hezekiah's day, and smote the remaining Amalekites at mount Seir.",
    context:"Tucked inside Judah's and Simeon's registers, the prayer of Jabez stands out as a marker of faith in a name-by-name record.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"1 Chronicles 4:9", text:"And Jabez was more honourable than his brethren: and his mother called his name Jabez, saying, Because I bare him with sorrow." },
      { ref:"1 Chronicles 4:10", text:"And Jabez called on the God of Israel, saying, Oh that thou wouldest bless me indeed, and enlarge my coast, and that thine hand might be with me, and that thou wouldest keep me from evil, that it may not grieve me! And God granted him that which he requested." }
    ],
    takeaway:"The Lord honors the prayer of anyone who calls on Him for blessing, direction, and keeping from evil.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"What did God do in response to Jabez's prayer?", choices:["He granted him that which he requested","He sent a prophet to test him","He delayed the answer for seven years","He asked Jabez to offer a sacrifice first"], a:0, why:"The record closes the prayer with the simple note that God granted him that which he requested." },
      { q:"Why was Jabez given his name?", choices:["He was born left-handed","His mother bore him with sorrow","He was born during a famine","His father named him after a city"], a:1, why:"His mother called his name Jabez, saying, Because I bare him with sorrow." },
      { q:"In whose days did Simeon's men destroy the inhabitants found at the entrance of Gedor?", choices:["Saul's","David's","Hezekiah's","Jehoshaphat's"], a:2, why:"Those written by name came in the days of Hezekiah king of Judah and destroyed the habitations there, because there was pasture for their flocks." }
    ] },
  { n:5, summary:"The tribes east of Jordan come into view: Reuben, Gad, and half of Manasseh. The chapter explains that although Reuben was Israel's firstborn, he defiled his father's bed, so his birthright went to Joseph's sons, while Judah prevailed and of him came the chief ruler. Their genealogies were reckoned in the days of Jotham of Judah and Jeroboam of Israel. Forty-four thousand seven hundred and sixty fighting men made war with the Hagarites and their allies; because they cried to God in the battle and trusted in Him, He was entreated of them, and they took vast herds of camels, sheep, and asses. The chapter ends on a sad note: later these tribes went after the gods of the peoples of the land, and God stirred up Assyria's kings, who carried them away captive to Halah, Habor, Hara, and Gozan, where they remained to that day.",
    context:"Reuben, Gad, and half Manasseh settle east of Jordan, win their war by trusting God, and later lose their land to Assyria for idolatry.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"1 Chronicles 5:1", text:"Now the sons of Reuben the firstborn of Israel, (for he was the firstborn; but, forasmuch as he defiled his father's bed, his birthright was given unto the sons of Joseph the son of Israel: and the genealogy is not to be reckoned after the birthright." },
      { ref:"1 Chronicles 5:20", text:"And they were helped against them, and the Hagarites were delivered into their hand, and all that were with them: for they cried to God in the battle, and he was entreated of them; because they put their trust in him." },
      { ref:"1 Chronicles 5:26", text:"And the God of Israel stirred up the spirit of Pul king of Assyria, and the spirit of Tilgath-pilneser king of Assyria, and he carried them away, even the Reubenites, and the Gadites, and the half tribe of Manasseh, and brought them unto Halah, and Habor, and Hara, and to the river Gozan, unto this day." }
    ],
    takeaway:"Trust in God brings deliverance, but unfaithfulness can cost His people even their inheritance.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"After Reuben defiled his father's bed, whose sons received his birthright?", choices:["Judah's","Joseph's","Levi's","Benjamin's"], a:1, why:"The birthright was given unto the sons of Joseph the son of Israel, though Judah prevailed and of him came the chief ruler." },
      { q:"Why were the two and a half tribes delivered against the Hagarites?", choices:["Their chariots outnumbered the enemy","They cried to God in the battle and put their trust in Him","The Hagarites fled at the sight of them","Saul came to their aid"], a:2, why:"They were helped because they cried to God in the battle, and He was entreated of them because they put their trust in him." },
      { q:"Which empire carried the Reubenites, Gadites, and half tribe of Manasseh away captive?", choices:["Egypt","Babylon","Persia","Assyria"], a:3, why:"God stirred up Pul and Tilgath-pilneser kings of Assyria, who carried them to Halah, Habor, Hara, and the river Gozan." }
    ] },
  { n:6, summary:"Chapter six follows the tribe set apart for the sanctuary. Levi's three sons were Gershon, Kohath, and Merari, and the high-priestly line runs from Levi through Kohath and Amram to Aaron, Moses, and Miriam, then down the generations to Zadok and on to Jehozadak, who went into captivity when Nebuchadnezzar carried Judah and Jerusalem away. A later Azariah in the same line executed the priest's office in the temple Solomon built. The chapter also lists David's musicians appointed after the ark had rest — Heman the singer, Asaph at his right hand, and Ethan on the left — while other Levites served the tabernacle and Aaron's sons offered on the altars and made atonement for Israel. It closes with the cities and suburbs Israel gave the Levites by lot, including cities of refuge like Hebron and Shechem, thirteen cities for Aaron's sons.",
    context:"The priestly line from Levi and Aaron is traced to the captivity, and the Levites' musical service and inheritance cities are catalogued.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"1 Chronicles 6:15", text:"And Jehozadak went into captivity, when the LORD carried away Judah and Jerusalem by the hand of Nebuchadnezzar." },
      { ref:"1 Chronicles 6:31", text:"And these are they whom David set over the service of song in the house of the LORD, after that the ark had rest." },
      { ref:"1 Chronicles 6:49", text:"But Aaron and his sons offered upon the altar of the burnt offering, and on the altar of incense, and were appointed for all the work of the place most holy, and to make an atonement for Israel, according to all that Moses the servant of God had commanded." }
    ],
    takeaway:"God appoints and provides servants for every part of His worship, from sacrifice to song.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"Who were the three sons of Levi?", choices:["Gershon, Kohath, and Merari","Aaron, Moses, and Miriam","Nadab, Abihu, and Eleazar","Heman, Asaph, and Ethan"], a:0, why:"The chapter opens, The sons of Levi; Gershon, Kohath, and Merari." },
      { q:"Whom did David set over the service of song after the ark had rest?", choices:["Moses and Aaron","Samuel and his sons","Heman, Asaph, and Ethan","Chenaniah alone"], a:2, why:"Heman a singer of the Kohathites stood with his brother Asaph on his right hand and Ethan of Merari on the left." },
      { q:"In whose line does an Azariah appear who executed the priest's office in the temple Solomon built?", choices:["Nadab's","Elkanah's","Korah's","Johanan's"], a:3, why:"Johanan begat Azariah, who it is that executed the priest's office in the temple that Solomon built in Jerusalem." }
    ] },
  { n:7, summary:"Chapter seven completes the tribal registers with war-ready numbers counted in David's day. Issachar's fighting men were reckoned at 87,000; Benjamin's branches counted 22,034, then 20,200, then 17,200 soldiers fit for battle; Asher's able men numbered 26,000. Naphtali's four sons are listed briefly. In Manasseh's line come Machir the father of Gilead and Zelophehad, whose children were daughters. Ephraim's section tells a sorrowful story: men of Gath slew his sons Ezer and Elead because they came down to take away their cattle, Ephraim mourned many days, and the son born afterward was named Beriah, because it went evil with his house; his line ran down to Jehoshua, that is, Joshua. The chapter ends by locating the possessions of Ephraim and Manasseh around Beth-el, Shechem, and other towns.",
    context:"War rolls of the tribes are rounded out with family stories, including Ephraim's grief over slain sons and the birth of Beriah.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"1 Chronicles 7:2", text:"And the sons of Tola; Uzzi, and Rephaiah, and Jeriel, and Jahmai, and Jibsam, and Shemuel, heads of their father's house, to wit, of Tola: they were valiant men of might in their generations; whose number was in the days of David two and twenty thousand and six hundred." },
      { ref:"1 Chronicles 7:22", text:"And Ephraim their father mourned many days, and his brethren came to comfort him." },
      { ref:"1 Chronicles 7:40", text:"All these were the children of Asher, heads of their father's house, choice and mighty men of valour, chief of the princes. And the number throughout the genealogy of them that were apt to the war and to battle was twenty and six thousand men." }
    ],
    takeaway:"Even in long lists, Scripture remembers real grief and real hope in the story of God's people.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"Why was Ephraim's newborn son named Beriah?", choices:["Because it went evil with his house","Because he was born in Beth-el","Because he would be a mighty hunter","Because his mother wept for joy"], a:0, why:"After the deaths in the family, the son born to Ephraim was called Beriah, because it went evil with his house." },
      { q:"Which tribe's able warriors are numbered at twenty and six thousand?", choices:["Asher","Dan","Issachar","Zebulun"], a:1, why:"Verse 40 counts the children of Asher at twenty and six thousand men apt to the war and to battle." },
      { q:"What happened to Ephraim's sons Ezer and Elead?", choices:["They were slain by the men of Gath, who came to take away their cattle","They became judges in Israel","They led Israel out of Egypt","They built the city of Beth-horon"], a:3, why:"The men of Gath that were born in that land slew them, because they came down to take away their cattle." }
    ] },
  { n:8, summary:"Benjamin's genealogy fills this chapter, centered on his firstborn Bela's descendants and the families who lived in and around Jerusalem. Heads of fathers are listed from Geba, from Gibeon's neighborhood, and from towns like Ono, Lod, and Aijalon; Beriah and Shema are remembered as heads of the inhabitants of Aijalon who drove away the inhabitants of Gath. Many of these chief men dwelt in Jerusalem. The record's chief interest for the story to come is Saul's family line: Ner begat Kish, Kish begat Saul, and Saul's sons included Jonathan, Malchi-shua, Abinadab, and Esh-baal, with Jonathan's son Merib-baal and grandson Micah following. The chapter closes with Azel's six sons and the mighty archers among Ulam's sons — a hundred and fifty sons and grandsons — all counted among the sons of Benjamin.",
    context:"Benjamin's register names chiefs dwelling at Jerusalem and Gibeon and traces the family line of King Saul, whose story the book retells next.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"1 Chronicles 8:28", text:"These were heads of the fathers, by their generations, chief men. These dwelt in Jerusalem." },
      { ref:"1 Chronicles 8:33", text:"And Ner begat Kish, and Kish begat Saul, and Saul begat Jonathan, and Malchi-shua, and Abinadab, and Esh-baal." },
      { ref:"1 Chronicles 8:40", text:"And the sons of Ulam were mighty men of valour, archers, and had many sons, and sons' sons, an hundred and fifty. All these are of the sons of Benjamin." }
    ],
    takeaway:"Faithful records of names and places show that every family counts in God's unfolding plan.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"Whose family line runs from Ner through Kish in this chapter?", choices:["David's","Saul's","Samuel's","Solomon's"], a:1, why:"Ner begat Kish, and Kish begat Saul, whose sons and grandsons follow in the record." },
      { q:"Which two Benjamite heads drove away the inhabitants of Gath?", choices:["Beriah and Shema","Ehud and Gera","Shaharaim and Elpaal","Ner and Kish"], a:0, why:"Beriah and Shema were heads of the fathers of the inhabitants of Aijalon, who drove away the inhabitants of Gath." },
      { q:"Who was the son of Jonathan named in Benjamin's line?", choices:["Malchi-shua","Esh-baal","Merib-baal","Micah"], a:2, why:"The son of Jonathan was Merib-baal, and Merib-baal begat Micah." }
    ] },
  { n:9, summary:"The register turns to the people who actually lived in Jerusalem after the return from Babylon. All Israel had been reckoned by genealogies and written in the book of the kings of Israel and Judah, having been carried away for their transgression. The first to dwell again in their cities were Israelites, priests, Levites, and Nethinims, from Judah, Benjamin, Ephraim, and Manasseh — including 690 of Zerah's line and 956 Benjamite chief fathers. Priests, Levites, and porters fill the chapter: 212 porters chosen for the gates, ordained by David and Samuel; keepers of the ministering vessels, the fine flour, wine, oil, and spices; makers of the ointment and shewbread; and singers employed in the work day and night, for the opening of the house every morning pertained to them. The chapter closes by repeating Saul's line from Gibeon.",
    context:"Written for the returned community, this register shows who resettled Jerusalem and who kept the temple's gates, vessels, and songs.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"1 Chronicles 9:1", text:"So all Israel were reckoned by genealogies; and, behold, they were written in the book of the kings of Israel and Judah, who were carried away to Babylon for their transgression." },
      { ref:"1 Chronicles 9:22", text:"All these which were chosen to be porters in the gates were two hundred and twelve. These were reckoned by their genealogy in their villages, whom David and Samuel the seer did ordain in their set office." },
      { ref:"1 Chronicles 9:27", text:"And they lodged round about the house of God, because the charge was upon them, and the opening thereof every morning pertained to them." }
    ],
    takeaway:"God's house runs on quiet, daily faithfulness — gates watched, vessels counted, bread prepared, songs sung.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"How many porters were chosen for the gates of the house of the LORD?", choices:["One hundred and forty-four","Two hundred and twelve","Three hundred","Six hundred and ninety"], a:1, why:"All chosen to be porters in the gates were two hundred and twelve." },
      { q:"Who ordained the porters in their set office?", choices:["Moses and Aaron","Solomon and Nathan","David and Samuel the seer","Zerubbabel and Jeshua"], a:2, why:"The porters were reckoned by genealogy, whom David and Samuel the seer did ordain in their set office." },
      { q:"Why did the singers stay in the chambers, free from other duties?", choices:["They were employed in that work day and night","They were forbidden to touch the vessels","They were too old for heavier work","The king required them at court"], a:3, why:"Remaining in the chambers they were free, for they were employed in that work day and night." }
    ] },
  { n:10, summary:"The book now retells the death of King Saul. The Philistines fought against Israel at mount Gilboa; the men of Israel fled and fell slain, and Jonathan, Abinadab, and Malchi-shua, Saul's three sons, died with him. Wounded by archers and unwilling to let uncircumcised Philistines abuse him, Saul fell on his own sword, and his armourbearer died likewise, so all his house died together. The Philistines stripped the bodies, put Saul's armour in the house of their gods, and fastened his head in the temple of Dagon. Valiant men of Jabesh-gilead rescued the bodies, buried the bones under the oak, and fasted seven days. The chapter's verdict is sober: Saul died for his transgression against the word of the LORD, for asking counsel of one that had a familiar spirit, and because he inquired not of the LORD; the kingdom turned to David the son of Jesse.",
    context:"The retelling of David's reign begins at its turning point: Saul's death on mount Gilboa and the transfer of the kingdom to David.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"1 Chronicles 10:4", text:"Then said Saul to his armourbearer, Draw thy sword, and thrust me through therewith; lest these uncircumcised come and abuse me. But his armourbearer would not; for he was sore afraid. So Saul took a sword, and fell upon it." },
      { ref:"1 Chronicles 10:13", text:"So Saul died for his transgression which he committed against the LORD, even against the word of the LORD, which he kept not, and also for asking counsel of one that had a familiar spirit, to inquire of it;" },
      { ref:"1 Chronicles 10:14", text:"And inquired not of the LORD: therefore he slew him, and turned the kingdom unto David the son of Jesse." }
    ],
    takeaway:"Victory and position cannot save the person who will not keep or seek the word of the LORD.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"Where did Saul and his sons fall before the Philistines?", choices:["Mount Gilboa","The valley of Rephaim","The plains of Moab","The hill of Ammah"], a:0, why:"The men of Israel fled and fell down slain in mount Gilboa, where Saul and his three sons died." },
      { q:"What was done with Saul's armour and head after his death?", choices:["Buried with honor at Jabesh","Kept by Samuel the seer","Burned in the valley","His armour was put in the house of their gods and his head fastened in the temple of Dagon"], a:2, why:"The Philistines put his armour in the house of their gods and fastened his head in the temple of Dagon." },
      { q:"To whom did the LORD turn the kingdom after Saul's death?", choices:["Saul's son Jonathan","Samuel the seer","Abner the captain of the host","David the son of Jesse"], a:3, why:"Because Saul inquired not of the LORD, He slew him and turned the kingdom unto David the son of Jesse." }
    ] },
  { n:11, summary:"All Israel gathers to David at Hebron, acknowledging that he led them out and in even under Saul, and that the LORD said he should feed Israel as ruler; the elders make a covenant and anoint him king, according to the word of the LORD by Samuel. David then takes Jerusalem: the Jebusites taunt him, but Joab goes up first and becomes chief, the castle of Zion becomes the city of David, and David grows greater because the LORD of hosts was with him. The chapter lists his mighty men: Jashobeam, who lifted his spear against three hundred slain at one time; Eleazar, who defended a parcel of ground full of barley at Pas-dammim; and Benaiah, who slew two lionlike men of Moab, a lion in a pit on a snowy day, and a giant Egyptian with his own spear. Three mighties broke through Philistine lines for water from Bethlehem's well, which David poured out to the LORD because their lives were at stake.",
    context:"United Israel covenants with David at Hebron, he captures Zion, and his mighty men are honored for their valor and devotion.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"1 Chronicles 11:3", text:"Therefore came all the elders of Israel to the king to Hebron; and David made a covenant with them in Hebron before the LORD; and they anointed David king over Israel, according to the word of the LORD by Samuel." },
      { ref:"1 Chronicles 11:5", text:"And the inhabitants of Jebus said to David, Thou shalt not come hither. Nevertheless David took the castle of Zion, which is the city of David." },
      { ref:"1 Chronicles 11:19", text:"And said, My God forbid it me, that I should do this thing: shall I drink the blood of these men that have put their lives in jeopardy? for with the jeopardy of their lives they brought it. Therefore he would not drink it. These things did these three mightiest." }
    ],
    takeaway:"True greatness is measured in devotion — strength offered for a king and gifts poured out to the LORD.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"Who went up first against the Jebusites and became chief and captain?", choices:["Joab the son of Zeruiah","Abishai","Benaiah","Jashobeam"], a:0, why:"David promised the honor to whoever smote the Jebusites first, and Joab went first up and was chief." },
      { q:"What did David do with the water brought from the well of Bethlehem?", choices:["He drank it with his captains","He poured it out to the LORD","He kept it in the tabernacle","He gave it to the thirty"], a:1, why:"David would not drink it, saying he could not drink the blood of men who put their lives in jeopardy, and poured it out to the LORD." },
      { q:"Which mighty man killed a lion in a pit on a snowy day and an Egyptian of great stature?", choices:["Jashobeam","Eleazar","Abishai","Benaiah"], a:3, why:"Benaiah the son of Jehoiada slew two lionlike men of Moab, a lion in a pit in a snowy day, and an Egyptian five cubits high with his own spear." }
    ] },
  { n:12, summary:"This chapter celebrates the fighting men who rallied to David before and after Saul's fall. At Ziklag came skilled Benjamite archers who could use either hand, Gadites with faces like the faces of lions, swift as roes upon the mountains, who crossed Jordan in the first month when it had overflown all its banks, and men of Manasseh; when Amasai arrived, the spirit came upon him to declare, Thine are we, David, and on thy side. Day by day helpers came until it was a great host, like the host of God. The second half numbers the armed bands that came to Hebron to turn the kingdom of Saul to David: 6,800 of Judah, 7,100 of Simeon, 4,600 Levites, 3,000 Benjamites, 20,800 Ephraimites, 50,000 Zebulunites expert in war who were not of a double heart, and 120,000 from beyond Jordan. After three days of feasting, Israel made David king with one heart, and there was joy in Israel.",
    context:"Warriors from every tribe stream to David at Ziklag and Hebron, gathering the nation with one heart to make him king.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"1 Chronicles 12:18", text:"Then the spirit came upon Amasai, who was chief of the captains, and he said, Thine are we, David, and on thy side, thou son of Jesse: peace, peace be unto thee, and peace be to thine helpers; for thy God helpeth thee. Then David received them, and made them captains of the band." },
      { ref:"1 Chronicles 12:32", text:"And of the children of Issachar, which were men that had understanding of the times, to know what Israel ought to do; the heads of them were two hundred; and all their brethren were at their commandment." },
      { ref:"1 Chronicles 12:38", text:"All these men of war, that could keep rank, came with a perfect heart to Hebron, to make David king over all Israel: and all the rest also of Israel were of one heart to make David king." }
    ],
    takeaway:"When God's chosen leader stands, a whole heart unites people of every tribe and skill to his side.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"Which tribe's men had understanding of the times, to know what Israel ought to do?", choices:["Issachar","Gad","Dan","Naphtali"], a:0, why:"The children of Issachar were men that had understanding of the times; their two hundred heads led, and all their brethren were at their commandment." },
      { q:"What did Amasai declare when the spirit came upon him?", choices:["The kingdom belongs to Saul for ever","Thine are we, David, and on thy side; peace, peace be unto thee","We come to spy out the land for the Philistines","Turn back, for the LORD is against thee"], a:1, why:"Amasai's Spirit-given declaration pledged loyalty and peace to David, because thy God helpeth thee." },
      { q:"How many Zebulunites expert in war, not of a double heart, came to Hebron?", choices:["Fifty thousand","Eighteen thousand","Seven thousand one hundred","Twenty and eight thousand six hundred"], a:2, why:"Of Zebulun there were fifty thousand expert in war, which could keep rank, and which were not of a double heart." }
    ] },
  { n:13, summary:"David consults his captains and the whole congregation, and all agree the thing is right: let us bring again the ark of our God, for it was not sought in the days of Saul. All Israel gathers from Shihor of Egypt to Hemath and goes to Kirjath-jearim to carry the ark home. They set it on a new cart driven by Uzza and Ahio, and David and all Israel play before God with singing, harps, psalteries, timbrels, cymbals, and trumpets. At the threshingfloor of Chidon the oxen stumble; Uzza steadies the ark with his hand, and the anger of the LORD kindles against him — he dies there before God, and the place is called Perez-uzza. Afraid, David asks how the ark can come to him and leaves it in the house of Obed-edom the Gittite, where the LORD blesses his house and all that he has for three months.",
    context:"A joyful national attempt to bring the ark to Jerusalem ends in Uzza's death, and the ark waits at Obed-edom's house.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"1 Chronicles 13:3", text:"And let us bring again the ark of our God to us: for we inquired not at it in the days of Saul." },
      { ref:"1 Chronicles 13:10", text:"And the anger of the LORD was kindled against Uzza, and he smote him, because he put his hand to the ark: and there he died before God." },
      { ref:"1 Chronicles 13:14", text:"And the ark of God remained with the family of Obed-edom in his house three months. And the LORD blessed the house of Obed-edom, and all that he had." }
    ],
    takeaway:"Good intentions cannot replace obedience; holy things must be handled in the way God has appointed.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"From where did David bring the ark of God?", choices:["Shiloh","Kirjath-jearim (Baalah)","Gibeah","Gibeon"], a:1, why:"David went up with all Israel to Baalah, that is, Kirjath-jearim, to bring up thence the ark of God." },
      { q:"Why was the anger of the LORD kindled against Uzza?", choices:["He put his hand to the ark when the oxen stumbled","He stole part of the offering","He drove the cart carelessly into a ditch","He mocked the singers"], a:2, why:"When the oxen stumbled at the threshingfloor of Chidon, Uzza put forth his hand to hold the ark, and he died there before God." },
      { q:"What happened to Obed-edom's house while the ark stood there?", choices:["It was struck with plagues","It was robbed by Philistines","It was consumed by fire","The LORD blessed it and all that he had"], a:3, why:"The ark remained three months with Obed-edom's family, and the LORD blessed his house and all that he had." }
    ] },
  { n:14, summary:"David's kingdom is confirmed on every side. Hiram king of Tyre sends cedar timber, masons, and carpenters to build David a house; David perceives that the LORD has confirmed him king over Israel, lifting up his kingdom because of His people. More children are born to him in Jerusalem, including Shammua, Shobab, Nathan, and Solomon. When the Philistines hear he is anointed king over all Israel and spread out in the valley of Rephaim, David inquires of God, is told to go up, and smites them at Baal-perazim, saying God has broken forth upon his enemies like the breaking forth of waters; the idols they leave behind are burned with fire. When they raid again, God directs an ambush by the mulberry trees, promising to go forth before him; David obeys, smites them from Gibeon even to Gazer, and his fame and the fear of him go out into all lands.",
    context:"Confirmed as king, David inquires of God before each Philistine battle and wins victories that spread his fame among the nations.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"1 Chronicles 14:2", text:"And David perceived that the LORD had confirmed him king over Israel, for his kingdom was lifted up on high, because of his people Israel." },
      { ref:"1 Chronicles 14:15", text:"And it shall be, when thou shalt hear a sound of going in the tops of the mulberry trees, that then thou shalt go out to battle: for God is gone forth before thee to smite the host of the Philistines." },
      { ref:"1 Chronicles 14:17", text:"And the fame of David went out into all lands; and the LORD brought the fear of him upon all nations." }
    ],
    takeaway:"Ask God before you act: He directs those who inquire and goes before those who obey.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"What did Hiram king of Tyre send David?", choices:["Timber of cedars with masons and carpenters","A thousand talents of silver","Chariots and horsemen","Priests from Gibeon"], a:0, why:"Hiram sent messengers to David, and timber of cedars, with masons and carpenters, to build him an house." },
      { q:"What did David command to be done with the idols the Philistines left at Baal-perazim?", choices:["They were hidden in the tabernacle","They were returned to the Philistines","They were burned with fire","They were set up in Zion"], a:2, why:"When the Philistines left their gods there, David gave a commandment, and they were burned with fire." },
      { q:"What signal told David to go out to battle in the second encounter?", choices:["A trumpet blown by the priests","A sound of going in the tops of the mulberry trees","The ark carried before the host","A shout from Joab"], a:3, why:"God said that when David heard a sound of going in the tops of the mulberry trees, then he should go out, for God had gone forth before him." }
    ] },
  { n:15, summary:"David prepares a place for the ark and pitches a tent for it, then gathers all Israel to Jerusalem to bring the ark up properly. None, he says, ought to carry the ark but the Levites, whom the LORD chose for that ministry; the breach at the first came because Israel did not seek God after the due order. So Zadok and Abiathar, with the chief Levites, sanctify themselves, and the Levites bear the ark on their shoulders with the staves, as Moses commanded. David appoints singers — Heman, Asaph, and Ethan sounding cymbals of brass, with psalteries, harps, and trumpets, and Chenaniah instructing the song because he was skilful. Seven bullocks and seven rams are offered, David wears a robe of fine linen with an ephod of linen, and all Israel brings up the ark with shouting and instruments. When Michal, Saul's daughter, sees the king dancing and playing, she despises him in her heart.",
    context:"Learning from the breach at first, David reorders the ark's journey exactly as the law required, with sanctified Levites and appointed singers.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"1 Chronicles 15:2", text:"Then David said, None ought to carry the ark of God but the Levites: for them hath the LORD chosen to carry the ark of God, and to minister unto him for ever." },
      { ref:"1 Chronicles 15:13", text:"For because ye did it not at the first, the LORD our God made a breach upon us, for that we sought him not after the due order." },
      { ref:"1 Chronicles 15:29", text:"And it came to pass, as the ark of the covenant of the LORD came to the city of David, that Michal the daughter of Saul looking out at a window saw king David dancing and playing: and she despised him in her heart." }
    ],
    takeaway:"When we learn where we went wrong, wisdom is to return and do it God's way, in order and with joy.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"Who alone ought to carry the ark of God, according to David?", choices:["The Levites, whom the LORD chose","The king's guard","The elders of Israel","The priests' firstborn sons"], a:0, why:"David said none ought to carry the ark but the Levites, whom the LORD chose to carry it and minister unto him for ever." },
      { q:"How did the Levites carry the ark, as Moses commanded?", choices:["On a new cart drawn by oxen","On their shoulders with the staves thereon","Wrapped in badgers' skins on poles","On a sledge of cedar"], a:1, why:"The children of the Levites bare the ark of God upon their shoulders with the staves thereon, as Moses commanded according to the word of the LORD." },
      { q:"Which three were appointed to sound with cymbals of brass?", choices:["Chenaniah, Berechiah, and Elkanah","Zechariah, Aziel, and Jehiel","Obed-edom and Jehiah","Heman, Asaph, and Ethan"], a:3, why:"The singers Heman, Asaph, and Ethan were appointed to sound with cymbals of brass." }
    ] },
  { n:16, summary:"The ark is set in the tent David pitched, offerings are made, and David blesses the people in the name of the LORD, dealing to everyone a loaf of bread, a good piece of flesh, and a flagon of wine. Levites are appointed to minister, record, thank, and praise before the ark, with Asaph as chief. Then David delivers a psalm of thanksgiving to Asaph and his brethren. It calls on all the earth to give thanks, sing, and remember God's marvellous works and judgments; to be mindful always of His covenant with Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob — an everlasting covenant promising Canaan as their inheritance; to declare His glory among the heathen, for the LORD made the heavens while all other gods are idols; and to worship the LORD in the beauty of holiness, saying among the nations, The LORD reigneth. The people answer Amen and praise the LORD, and Asaph's family, porters, and the priests at Gibeon's high place are left to minister continually, for His mercy endureth for ever.",
    context:"With the ark at rest in Zion, David appoints daily worship and delivers a great psalm of thanksgiving for the occasion.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"1 Chronicles 16:8", text:"Give thanks unto the LORD, call upon his name, make known his deeds among the people." },
      { ref:"1 Chronicles 16:29", text:"Give unto the LORD the glory due unto his name: bring an offering, and come before him: worship the LORD in the beauty of holiness." },
      { ref:"1 Chronicles 16:34", text:"O give thanks unto the LORD; for he is good; for his mercy endureth for ever." }
    ],
    takeaway:"Remember God's covenant mercies, declare His glory to all nations, and worship the LORD who reigneth.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"Into whose hand did David deliver the psalm of thanksgiving?", choices:["Asaph and his brethren","Zadok and Abiathar","The porters of the gate","Chenaniah the master of song"], a:0, why:"On that day David delivered first this psalm to thank the LORD into the hand of Asaph and his brethren." },
      { q:"Which covenant does the psalm tell Israel to be mindful of?", choices:["A covenant made at mount Sinai's altar","The covenant made with Abraham, confirmed to Isaac, and to Jacob for an everlasting covenant","A covenant made with king David","The covenant of the sabbath"], a:1, why:"The psalm recalls the covenant made with Abraham, His oath to Isaac, confirmed to Jacob for a law and to Israel for an everlasting covenant." },
      { q:"What did David deal to every one of Israel when he blessed the people?", choices:["A loaf of bread, and a good piece of flesh, and a flagon of wine","A shekel of silver apiece","Garments of fine linen","A portion of the shewbread"], a:2, why:"To everyone, both man and woman, David gave a loaf of bread, a good piece of flesh, and a flagon of wine." }
    ] },
  { n:17, summary:"Resting in his house of cedars while the ark sits under curtains, David tells Nathan the prophet that he means to build a house for the LORD. Nathan approves, but that night the word of God reverses the plan: David will not build; the LORD, who has gone from tent to tent with Israel, will build David a house. He recalls taking David from the sheepcote to be ruler over Israel, promises to subdue his enemies, and vows to raise up David's seed after him, establishing his kingdom and throne for ever and saying, I will be his father, and he shall be my son. Nathan reports the vision, and David humbly sits before the LORD — Who am I, and what is mine house? — praying that the promise to his house stand established for ever and that the LORD's name be magnified, for thou blessest, O LORD, and it shall be blessed for ever.",
    context:"When David offers to build a house for the LORD, God instead covenants to establish the throne of David's son for ever.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"1 Chronicles 17:4", text:"Go and tell David my servant, Thus saith the LORD, Thou shalt not build me an house to dwell in:" },
      { ref:"1 Chronicles 17:14", text:"But I will settle him in mine house and in my kingdom for ever: and his throne shall be established for evermore." },
      { ref:"1 Chronicles 17:16", text:"And David the king came and sat before the LORD, and said, Who am I, O LORD God, and what is mine house, that thou hast brought me hitherto?" }
    ],
    takeaway:"When we offer to build something for God, He often answers with a greater promise to build for us.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"Who first told David to go ahead, before the word of the LORD came at night?", choices:["Gad the seer","Nathan the prophet","Zadok the priest","Abiathar the priest"], a:1, why:"Nathan first said, Do all that is in thine heart; for God is with thee — then the word of God came to him that same night." },
      { q:"What did the LORD promise that David's son would do?", choices:["Build the house and have his throne established for ever","Rule all the nations with a rod of iron","Restore the ark to Moses' tabernacle","Write the songs of Zion"], a:2, why:"He shall build me an house, said the LORD, and I will stablish his throne for ever, settling him in God's house and kingdom for evermore." },
      { q:"How did David respond to the covenant promise?", choices:["He doubted it would come to pass","He asked Nathan for a sign","He began gathering cedar and stone at once","He sat before the LORD in humble prayer, asking, Who am I?"], a:3, why:"David the king came and sat before the LORD, wondering who he was and what his house, that God had brought him so far." }
    ] },
  { n:18, summary:"David's wars secure the kingdom. He subdues the Philistines, taking Gath and its towns; Moab becomes his servants, bringing gifts; and Hadarezer king of Zobah is smitten toward the Euphrates, losing a thousand chariots, seven thousand horsemen, and twenty thousand footmen, whose chariot horses David houghed, keeping only a hundred chariots. When Syrians of Damascus come to Hadarezer's help, David slays twenty-two thousand of them, sets garrisons in Damascus, and the Syrians bring gifts. David carries the shields of gold to Jerusalem and very much brass from Tibhath and Chun — the brass wherewith Solomon later made the brasen sea, the pillars, and the vessels. Tou king of Hamath sends his son Hadoram with vessels of gold, silver, and brass to congratulate David; all is dedicated to the LORD, with spoils from Edom, Moab, Ammon, the Philistines, and Amalek — including Abishai's eighteen thousand Edomites slain in the valley of salt. Officers are listed, and David reigns over all Israel executing judgment and justice.",
    context:"David's wars subdue surrounding nations, and the spoils he dedicates to the LORD later supply material for Solomon's temple.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"1 Chronicles 18:8", text:"Likewise from Tibhath, and from Chun, cities of Hadarezer, brought David very much brass, wherewith Solomon made the brasen sea, and the pillars, and the vessels of brass." },
      { ref:"1 Chronicles 18:11", text:"Them also king David dedicated unto the LORD, with the silver and the gold that he brought from all these nations; from Edom, and from Moab, and from the children of Ammon, and from the Philistines, and from Amalek." },
      { ref:"1 Chronicles 18:14", text:"So David reigned over all Israel, and executed judgment and justice among all his people." }
    ],
    takeaway:"Whatever victories God grants, dedicate the spoils back to Him and His house.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"Whom did Tou king of Hamath send to David after the defeat of Hadarezer?", choices:["His son Hadoram","His captain Shophach","His brother Joram","His scribe Shavsha"], a:0, why:"Tou sent Hadoram his son to inquire of David's welfare and congratulate him, with vessels of gold and silver and brass." },
      { q:"What did David do with the shields of gold and the silver and gold from the nations?", choices:["Divided them among his mighty men","Dedicated them unto the LORD","Sent them to Hiram of Tyre","Stored them in his own house"], a:2, why:"David took the shields of gold to Jerusalem and dedicated them, with the silver and gold from all these nations, unto the LORD." },
      { q:"How many Syrians did David slay when they came to help Hadarezer?", choices:["Twelve thousand","Eighteen thousand","Forty thousand","Twenty and two thousand"], a:3, why:"When the Syrians of Damascus came to help Hadarezer, David slew of the Syrians two and twenty thousand men." }
    ] },
  { n:19, summary:"When Nahash king of Ammon dies, David sends messengers to comfort his son Hanun, remembering Nahash's kindness to him. Hanun's princes suspect spying, so Hanun shaves the messengers and cuts off their garments, sending them away humiliated; David has them tarry at Jericho till their beards grow. Realizing they have made themselves odious to David, the Ammonites hire thirty-two thousand chariots and horsemen from Mesopotamia, Syria-maachah, and Zobah. David sends Joab and the host of the mighty men. Facing Ammon before the city and the hired kings behind, Joab divides the army with Abishai and urges courage: behave valiantly for our people and the cities of our God, and let the LORD do that which is good in his sight. The Syrians flee, then Ammon; when the Syrians regroup beyond the river under Shophach, David himself crosses Jordan, slays seven thousand chariot fighters and forty thousand footmen, kills the captain, and the Syrians make peace, helping Ammon no more.",
    context:"A kindness misjudged by Ammon plunges the region into war, which David and Joab win on two fronts against Ammon and hired Syrians.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"1 Chronicles 19:2", text:"And David said, I will shew kindness unto Hanun the son of Nahash, because his father shewed kindness to me. And David sent messengers to comfort him concerning his father. So the servants of David came into the land of the children of Ammon to Hanun, to comfort him." },
      { ref:"1 Chronicles 19:13", text:"Be of good courage, and let us behave ourselves valiantly for our people, and for the cities of our God: and let the LORD do that which is good in his sight." },
      { ref:"1 Chronicles 19:19", text:"And when the servants of Hadarezer saw that they were put to the worse before Israel, they made peace with David, and became his servants: neither would the Syrians help the children of Ammon any more." }
    ],
    takeaway:"Do your part valiantly for God's people and cities, and let the LORD do that which is good in His sight.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"How did Hanun treat David's comforters?", choices:["He shaved them and cut off their garments in the midst","He feasted them in his palace","He cast them into prison","He sent them home with gifts"], a:0, why:"Suspecting them of spying, Hanun took David's servants, shaved them, and cut off their garments, sending them away in shame." },
      { q:"What arrangement did Joab make when the battle was set before and behind?", choices:["He retreated across Jordan","He chose the best men against the Syrians and gave the rest to Abishai against Ammon, promising mutual help","He waited for David to arrive before fighting","He sent messengers offering tribute"], a:1, why:"Joab arrayed his choice men against the Syrians, delivered the rest to Abishai against Ammon, and pledged that whichever side was too strong, the other would help." },
      { q:"What did the Syrians do after their second defeat?", choices:["They made peace with David and became his servants","They joined Ammon for another campaign","They fled into Egypt","They laid siege to Jerusalem"], a:3, why:"The servants of Hadarezer made peace with David and became his servants, and would help the children of Ammon no more." }
    ] },
  { n:20, summary:"When the season for campaigning came, Joab led out the army, wasted the country of the children of Ammon, and besieged Rabbah while David tarried at Jerusalem. Rabbah is smitten and destroyed, and David takes the captured king's crown — a talent of gold with precious stones — sets it on his own head, and brings exceeding much spoil out of the city; the people of the land are dealt with severely, and David and all the people return to Jerusalem. The chapter then recalls the fall of Philistine warriors descended from the giants: Sibbechai the Hushathite slew Sippai at Gezer; Elhanan the son of Jair slew Lahmi the brother of Goliath the Gittite, whose spear staff was like a weaver's beam; and at Gath a man of great stature with twenty-four fingers and toes, six on each hand and foot, defied Israel and was slain by Jonathan the son of Shimea, David's brother. These fell by the hand of David and by the hand of his servants.",
    context:"The Ammonite war ends at Rabbah, and brief battle notices record the fall of Philistine giants by David's servants.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"1 Chronicles 20:2", text:"And David took the crown of their king from off his head, and found it to weigh a talent of gold, and there were precious stones in it; and it was set upon David's head: and he brought also exceeding much spoil out of the city." },
      { ref:"1 Chronicles 20:5", text:"And there was war again with the Philistines; and Elhanan the son of Jair slew Lahmi the brother of Goliath the Gittite, whose spear staff was like a weaver's beam." },
      { ref:"1 Chronicles 20:8", text:"These were born unto the giant in Gath; and they fell by the hand of David, and by the hand of his servants." }
    ],
    takeaway:"The Lord's work advances through His appointed servants as well as famous leaders.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"Who slew Lahmi the brother of Goliath the Gittite?", choices:["Elhanan the son of Jair","Sibbechai the Hushathite","Jonathan the son of Shimea","Abishai the son of Zeruiah"], a:0, why:"In the war again with the Philistines, Elhanan the son of Jair slew Lahmi, whose spear staff was like a weaver's beam." },
      { q:"What was remarkable about the crown taken from the king of Rabbah?", choices:["It was made of iron and brass","It weighed a talent of gold and held precious stones","It had never been worn by any king","It was covered with mirrors of brass"], a:1, why:"The crown of their king was found to weigh a talent of gold, with precious stones in it, and it was set upon David's head." },
      { q:"Who killed the giant of Gath that had six fingers on each hand and six toes on each foot?", choices:["David himself","Benaiah the son of Jehoiada","Joab the captain","Jonathan the son of Shimea, David's brother"], a:2, why:"When the man of great stature defied Israel, Jonathan the son of Shimea David's brother slew him." }
    ] },
  { n:21, summary:"Satan provokes David to number Israel, and David commands Joab to count the men from Beer-sheba to Dan. Joab protests — why will my lord be a cause of trespass to Israel? — but the king's word prevails, though Joab leaves Levi and Benjamin uncounted. God is displeased with the thing and smites Israel. David confesses he has sinned greatly and done very foolishly, and through Gad the seer the LORD offers three choices: three years' famine, three months of fleeing before enemies, or three days of pestilence. David chooses to fall into the hand of the LORD, whose mercies are very great. Seventy thousand die; as the destroying angel reaches Jerusalem, God says, It is enough, stay now thine hand, and the angel stands by the threshingfloor of Ornan the Jebusite. David takes the blame for his sheep, buys Ornan's floor for six hundred shekels of gold, builds an altar, and the LORD answers him by fire from heaven, and the angel sheathes his sword.",
    context:"David's sinful census brings pestilence on Israel, but the judgment stops at Ornan's threshingfloor, which becomes the site of the altar.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"1 Chronicles 21:1", text:"And Satan stood up against Israel, and provoked David to number Israel." },
      { ref:"1 Chronicles 21:17", text:"And David said unto God, Is it not I that commanded the people to be numbered? even I it is that have sinned and done evil indeed; but as for these sheep, what have they done? let thine hand, I pray thee, O LORD my God, be on me, and on my father's house; but not on thy people, that they should be plagued." },
      { ref:"1 Chronicles 21:24", text:"And king David said to Ornan, Nay; but I will verily buy it for the full price: for I will not take that which is thine for the LORD, nor offer burnt offerings without cost." }
    ],
    takeaway:"Sin brings real consequences, but confession and sacrifice at God's appointed place open the way to mercy.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"Which three judgments did the LORD set before David?", choices:["Three battles, three sieges, or three famines","Three years' famine, three months before foes, or three days of pestilence","Three plagues on herds, crops, or firstborn","Three years of exile, drought, or silence"], a:1, why:"Through Gad the LORD offered three years' famine, three months destroyed before foes, or three days with the sword of the LORD, even pestilence." },
      { q:"How much did David pay Ornan for the threshingfloor?", choices:["Fifty shekels of silver","Four hundred shekels of silver","Six hundred shekels of gold","One talent of gold"], a:2, why:"David gave to Ornan for the place six hundred shekels of gold by weight." },
      { q:"Why would David not take Ornan's place and oxen as a gift?", choices:["He feared the people would resent it","The law forbad gifts from Jebusites","Gad told him to buy from another","He would not offer burnt offerings without cost"], a:3, why:"David said he would not take what was Ornan's for the LORD, nor offer burnt offerings without cost." }
    ] },
  { n:22, summary:"David declares the threshingfloor the house of the LORD God and the altar for Israel, and begins assembling materials: strangers in the land hew wrought stones, iron and brass are gathered without weight, and the Zidonians and they of Tyre bring abundant cedar. Knowing Solomon is young and tender and the house must be extraordinarily glorious, of fame throughout all countries, David prepares abundantly before his death. He charges Solomon plainly: David's many wars and much shed blood barred him from building, but a son of rest, Solomon, would build the house, with the LORD establishing his throne over Israel for ever. David counsels him to keep the law of the LORD, to be strong and of good courage, and lists the vast provision — a hundred thousand talents of gold and a million of silver, workmen of every craft — then commands the princes of Israel to set their hearts to seek the LORD and arise and build the sanctuary.",
    context:"With the altar site chosen, David stockpiles temple materials in abundance and formally charges young Solomon to build the house of the LORD.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"1 Chronicles 22:5", text:"And David said, Solomon my son is young and tender, and the house that is to be builded for the LORD must be exceeding magnifical, of fame and of glory throughout all countries: I will therefore now make preparation for it. So David prepared abundantly before his death." },
      { ref:"1 Chronicles 22:9", text:"Behold, a son shall be born to thee, who shall be a man of rest; and I will give him rest from all his enemies round about: for his name shall be Solomon, and I will give peace and quietness unto Israel in his days." },
      { ref:"1 Chronicles 22:19", text:"Now set your heart and your soul to seek the LORD your God; arise therefore, and build ye the sanctuary of the LORD God, to bring the ark of the covenant of the LORD, and the holy vessels of God, into the house that is to be built to the name of the LORD." }
    ],
    takeaway:"Prepare faithfully in your season for work God may finish through another.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"Why did the LORD say David could not build the house?", choices:["He had shed much blood and made great wars","He was too old for the work","The people had rejected the plan","There was not enough gold in Israel"], a:0, why:"The word of the LORD came to David that because he had shed blood abundantly and made great wars, he would not build an house unto God's name." },
      { q:"What kind of man did the word of the LORD say Solomon would be?", choices:["A man of war like his father","A man of rest, with peace and quietness in his days","A wandering prophet","A builder of many cities"], a:2, why:"A son would be born who would be a man of rest; his name would be Solomon, and the LORD would give peace and quietness unto Israel in his days." },
      { q:"What did David command the princes of Israel to do?", choices:["Choose a new capital for the kingdom","Draft the strangers into the army","Send more cedar from Tyre","Set their heart and soul to seek the LORD, and arise and build the sanctuary"], a:3, why:"David told the princes the land was subdued and given them rest on every side, and charged them to seek the LORD and build the sanctuary to bring in the ark and holy vessels." }
    ] },
  { n:23, summary:"Old and full of days, David makes Solomon king and gathers the princes, priests, and Levites for a great reorganization of temple service. The Levites, numbered from thirty years and upward, total thirty-eight thousand: twenty-four thousand to set forward the work of the house of the LORD, six thousand as officers and judges, four thousand porters, and four thousand to praise the LORD with the instruments David made. They are divided into courses under Gershon, Kohath, and Merari, with family heads named. Aaron was separated to sanctify the most holy things, burn incense before the LORD, minister, and bless in His name for ever, while Moses' sons are reckoned among the Levites. Because God has given rest and the Levites need no more carry the tabernacle, their service is counted from twenty years upward: helping Aaron's sons, preparing shewbread and offerings, standing every morning and evening to thank and praise the LORD, and keeping the charges of the tabernacle, the holy place, and their brethren.",
    context:"In his final arrangements David crowns Solomon and reorganizes the Levites from tabernacle carriers into temple servants, officers, porters, and singers.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"1 Chronicles 23:1", text:"So when David was old and full of days, he made Solomon his son king over Israel." },
      { ref:"1 Chronicles 23:3", text:"Now the Levites were numbered from the age of thirty years and upward: and their number by their polls, man by man, was thirty and eight thousand." },
      { ref:"1 Chronicles 23:25", text:"For David said, The LORD God of Israel hath given rest unto his people, that they may dwell in Jerusalem for ever:" }
    ],
    takeaway:"When God gives rest, service changes form but never loses its purpose: praise, order, and daily faithfulness.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"How many Levites were numbered from thirty years and upward?", choices:["Twenty and four thousand","Thirty and eight thousand","Forty and two thousand","Fifty and six thousand"], a:0, why:"The Levites numbered from thirty years and upward totaled thirty and eight thousand." },
      { q:"Why did the Levites no longer need to carry the tabernacle?", choices:["They were too few in number","The LORD had given rest to His people, who were to dwell in Jerusalem for ever","Solomon's wagons replaced their shoulders","The tabernacle had been destroyed by war"], a:1, why:"David said the LORD had given rest unto His people to dwell in Jerusalem for ever, so the Levites should no more carry the tabernacle or its vessels." },
      { q:"Who among Levi's descendants was separated to sanctify the most holy things and burn incense before the LORD?", choices:["Moses","Gershon","Kohath","Aaron"], a:3, why:"Aaron was separated, he and his sons for ever, to sanctify the most holy things, burn incense, minister, and bless in the LORD's name for ever." }
    ] },
  { n:24, summary:"The priestly line is organized into twenty-four courses. Aaron's sons Nadab and Abihu died childless before their father, leaving Eleazar and Ithamar to execute the priest's office; David distributes their descendants under Zadok of Eleazar's line and Ahimelech of Ithamar's. More chief men are found of Eleazar — sixteen heads of fathers against eight of Ithamar — and all are divided by lot, recorded by Shemaiah the scribe before the king, the princes, and the priests. The first lot falls to Jehoiarib, the second to Jedaiah, and so on through the twenty-four houses to Maaziah; the eighth lot is Abijah. These orderings govern when each course comes into the house of the LORD, as the LORD God of Israel had commanded Aaron. The chapter closes with the rest of Levi's sons — from Amram's, Izhar's, Hebron's, Uzziel's, and Merari's lines — who likewise cast lots before David, Zadok, Ahimelech, and the chief fathers.",
    context:"David, Zadok, and Ahimelech arrange Aaron's descendants into twenty-four priestly courses by lot for service in the house of the LORD.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"1 Chronicles 24:2", text:"But Nadab and Abihu died before their father, and had no children: therefore Eleazar and Ithamar executed the priest's office." },
      { ref:"1 Chronicles 24:5", text:"Thus were they divided by lot, one sort with another; for the governors of the sanctuary, and governors of the house of God, were of the sons of Eleazar, and of the sons of Ithamar." },
      { ref:"1 Chronicles 24:19", text:"These were the orderings of them in their service to come into the house of the LORD, according to their manner, under Aaron their father, as the LORD God of Israel had commanded him." }
    ],
    takeaway:"God's house runs on fair, prayerful order — every family an appointed turn, every course its season.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"Why did only Eleazar and Ithamar execute the priest's office?", choices:["Nadab and Abihu died before their father and had no children","Nadab and Abihu were appointed scribes","Eleazar and Ithamar bought the office","The other sons served at Gibeon only"], a:0, why:"Nadab and Abihu died before their father and had no children, so Eleazar and Ithamar executed the priest's office." },
      { q:"Which house received the first lot?", choices:["Jedaiah","Jehoiarib","Harim","Maaziah"], a:1, why:"The first lot came forth to Jehoiarib, the second to Jedaiah, and the lots ran on through twenty-four courses." },
      { q:"How many chief men were found among the sons of Eleazar?", choices:["Eight","Ten","Sixteen","Twenty and four"], a:2, why:"Sixteen chief men of the house of their fathers were found among the sons of Eleazar, against eight among the sons of Ithamar." }
    ] },
  { n:25, summary:"David and the captains of the host set apart the sons of Asaph, Heman, and Jeduthun to prophesy with harps, psalteries, and cymbals in the service of song. Jeduthun's family gives thanks and praises with a harp; Heman, the king's seer in the words of God, receives fourteen sons and three daughters from God, and all serve under their father's direction according to the king's order. The number of those taught in the songs of the LORD, all that were cunning, is two hundred fourscore and eight — 288. Lots are cast, ward against ward, for the small as well as the great, the teacher as well as the scholar, and each of the twenty-four lots designates a house of twelve — the first to Joseph of Asaph's family, the last to Romamti-ezer. Music in Israel's worship is thus ordered, taught, and prophetic, filling the service of the house of the LORD with praise.",
    context:"Temple music is organized as a prophetic ministry under Asaph, Jeduthun, and Heman, with 288 skilled singers assigned by lots.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"1 Chronicles 25:1", text:"Moreover David and the captains of the host separated to the service of the sons of Asaph, and of Heman, and of Jeduthun, who should prophesy with harps, with psalteries, and with cymbals: and the number of the workmen according to their service was:" },
      { ref:"1 Chronicles 25:7", text:"So the number of them, with their brethren that were instructed in the songs of the LORD, even all that were cunning, was two hundred fourscore and eight." },
      { ref:"1 Chronicles 25:8", text:"And they cast lots, ward against ward, as well the small as the great, the teacher as the scholar." }
    ],
    takeaway:"Praise is both gift and craft: God's people train their talents and offer them in humble order.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"Whose families were separated for the service of song?", choices:["Heman, Ethan, and Chenaniah","Asaph, Jeduthun, and Heman","Asaph, Nathan, and Gad","Zadok, Abiathar, and Jehoiarib"], a:1, why:"David and the captains separated the sons of Asaph, of Heman, and of Jeduthun to the service of song." },
      { q:"What were these separated musicians to do with harps, psalteries, and cymbals?", choices:["Teach Israel to dance before the ark","March at the head of the armies","Prophesy in the service of song","Keep watch at the temple gates"], a:2, why:"They were separated who should prophesy with harps, with psalteries, and with cymbals." },
      { q:"How many were taught in the songs of the LORD, all that were cunning?", choices:["One hundred and forty-four","Two hundred and twelve","Three hundred and sixty","Two hundred fourscore and eight"], a:3, why:"The number with their brethren that were instructed in the songs of the LORD was two hundred fourscore and eight." }
    ] },
  { n:26, summary:"The gatekeepers are organized by divisions. From the Korhites, Meshelemiah's family gives strong men; Obed-edom's sons and brethren, blessed of God, number sixty-two able men for strength for the service; and Hosah's household yields thirteen, with Simri made chief though not the firstborn. Lots are cast for every gate, small and great alike: Shelemiah takes the east, his wise son Zechariah the north, Obed-edom the south with the house of Asuppim, and the west falls to Shuppim and Hosah at the gate Shallecheth; set numbers of Levites watch each quarter, with wards against ward. The chapter then lists treasurers: Ahijah over the treasures of the house of God; Shebuel, son of Gershom son of Moses, as ruler of treasures; and Shelomith and his brethren over the dedicated things — spoils won in battles by David, the chief fathers, and the captains, and all that Samuel, Saul, Abner, and Joab had dedicated. Officers and judges for Israel's outward business, and valiant Hebronites over the tribes beyond Jordan, complete the register.",
    context:"Gatekeepers receive their gates by lot, and treasurers and officers are appointed to guard the temple's treasures and administer the king's business.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"1 Chronicles 26:13", text:"And they cast lots, as well the small as the great, according to the house of their fathers, for every gate." },
      { ref:"1 Chronicles 26:27", text:"Out of the spoils won in battles did they dedicate to maintain the house of the LORD." },
      { ref:"1 Chronicles 26:31", text:"Among the Hebronites was Jerijah the chief, even among the Hebronites, according to the generations of his fathers. In the fortieth year of the reign of David they were sought for, and there were found among them mighty men of valour at Jazer of Gilead." }
    ],
    takeaway:"Guarding gates and treasuries is sacred work; faithfulness in small stations honors God's house.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"Which son of Gershom the son of Moses was ruler of the treasures?", choices:["Shebuel","Shelomith","Ahijah","Rehabiah"], a:0, why:"Shebuel the son of Gershom, the son of Moses, was ruler of the treasures." },
      { q:"Whence came the dedicated things kept to maintain the house of the LORD?", choices:["Yearly tithes of the harvest","Tribute from Egypt","Out of the spoils won in battles","Sale of the temple vessels"], a:2, why:"David, the chief fathers, the captains, and the captains of the host dedicated out of the spoils won in battles to maintain the house of the LORD." },
      { q:"How many able men of strength for the service belonged to Obed-edom?", choices:["Thirteen","Eighteen","Thirty and two","Threescore and two"], a:3, why:"The sons of Obed-edom, with their sons and brethren, able men for strength for the service, were threescore and two, for God blessed him." }
    ] },
  { n:27, summary:"Israel's army is arranged into twelve monthly courses of twenty-four thousand each, serving the king month by month: Jashobeam leads the first month, Dodai the second, Benaiah the third — the same who was mighty among the thirty — Asahel's line the fourth, and so on through Heldai in the twelfth. Over the tribes, princes are listed — Eliezer over Reuben, Zadok over the Aaronites, Elihu of David's brethren over Judah — but David takes no number of those twenty and under, for the LORD had said He would increase Israel like to the stars of the heavens; Joab's beginning to number brought wrath upon Israel, and it was never entered in the account of the chronicles of king David. The chapter also names stewards of the king's substance: treasurers, tillers of the ground, vinedressers and wine cellars, olive and sycomore keepers, oil cellars, herds in Sharon and the valleys, camels, asses, and flocks. David's counselors — Jonathan his uncle, Ahithophel, Hushai the companion, and Joab the general — end the roster.",
    context:"Twelve monthly army courses, tribal princes, and stewards over fields, herds, and treasuries display the administrative order of David's kingdom.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"1 Chronicles 27:1", text:"Now the children of Israel after their number, to wit, the chief fathers and captains of thousands and hundreds, and their officers that served the king in any matter of the courses, which came in and went out month by month throughout all the months of the year, of every course were twenty and four thousand." },
      { ref:"1 Chronicles 27:23", text:"But David took not the number of them from twenty years old and under: because the LORD had said he would increase Israel like to the stars of the heavens." },
      { ref:"1 Chronicles 27:24", text:"Joab the son of Zeruiah began to number, but he finished not, because there fell wrath for it against Israel; neither was the number put in the account of the chronicles of king David." }
    ],
    takeaway:"Order and stewardship are quiet forms of devotion that keep a kingdom ready to serve God.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"How many men served in each monthly course of Israel's army?", choices:["Twelve thousand","Twenty and four thousand","Thirty and six thousand","Fifty thousand"], a:0, why:"The courses came in and went out month by month, and of every course were twenty and four thousand." },
      { q:"Why did David not number those twenty years old and under?", choices:["Joab refused to count the young men","The LORD had said He would increase Israel like to the stars of the heavens","The records had been lost in the wars","The tribes refused to be counted"], a:1, why:"David took no number of them, because the LORD had said he would increase Israel like to the stars of the heavens." },
      { q:"Who was the general of the king's army, named at the chapter's end?", choices:["Joab","Abishai","Benaiah","Hushai"], a:3, why:"After listing the counselors, the record names Joab as the general of the king's army." }
    ] },
  { n:28, summary:"David assembles Israel's leaders and stands to speak. He wanted to build a house of rest for the ark, but God refused him because he was a man of war who had shed blood; yet the LORD chose his house, and of all his sons chose Solomon to sit on the throne of the kingdom, promising to establish his kingdom for ever if he remains constant in the commandments. David charges Israel to keep and seek the LORD's commandments so they may possess the good land and leave it an inheritance for their children, and charges Solomon: know the God of thy father, serve Him with a perfect heart and willing mind, for the LORD searcheth all hearts; if thou seek Him, He will be found of thee. Then David gives Solomon the pattern of the porch, courts, treasuries, and vessels — all made clear to him in writing by the LORD's hand — and closes with the command: be strong and of good courage, and do it, for God will not fail thee.",
    context:"David publicly hands the throne and the temple plan to Solomon, charging him and all Israel to seek the LORD wholeheartedly.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"1 Chronicles 28:5", text:"And of all my sons, (for the LORD hath given me many sons,) he hath chosen Solomon my son to sit upon the throne of the kingdom of the LORD over Israel." },
      { ref:"1 Chronicles 28:9", text:"And thou, Solomon my son, know thou the God of thy father, and serve him with a perfect heart and with a willing mind: for the LORD searcheth all hearts, and understandeth all the imaginations of the thoughts: if thou seek him, he will be found of thee; but if thou forsake him, he will cast thee off for ever." },
      { ref:"1 Chronicles 28:19", text:"All this, said David, the LORD made me understand in writing by his hand upon me, even all the works of this pattern." }
    ],
    takeaway:"The LORD searches all hearts; seek Him with a perfect heart and a willing mind, and He will be found.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"Why did God refuse David as the temple's builder?", choices:["He was a man of war and had shed blood","He lacked the skills of architecture","The congregation chose Solomon instead","Nathan had forbidden him to ask"], a:0, why:"God said to David, Thou shalt not build an house for my name, because thou hast been a man of war, and hast shed blood." },
      { q:"What does David say the LORD searches?", choices:["Only the outward deeds of kings","All hearts and the imaginations of the thoughts","The records of the tribes yearly","The deep places of the earth"], a:1, why:"David charged Solomon to serve God with a perfect heart and willing mind, for the LORD searcheth all hearts and understandeth all the imaginations of the thoughts." },
      { q:"How did David receive the knowledge of the temple's pattern?", choices:["From the writings of Moses","From Hiram's architects at Tyre","The LORD made him understand it in writing by his hand upon him","From a vision at Gibeon"], a:2, why:"David said the LORD made him understand all the works of the pattern in writing by his hand upon him." }
    ] },
  { n:29, summary:"For the house of God David has prepared gold, silver, brass, iron, wood, onyx and precious stones, and marble, adding from his own proper good three thousand talents of gold and seven thousand of refined silver. When he asks, Who then is willing to consecrate his service this day unto the LORD? the leaders offer willingly beyond all he had given: gold, silver, brass, iron, and precious stones. The people rejoice with a perfect heart, and David blesses the LORD before the congregation, confessing that all greatness, power, glory, victory, and majesty are His, and that all they gave was His own returned to Him. After sacrifices of a thousand bullocks, rams, and lambs, Solomon is made king a second time, anointed unto the LORD with Zadok as priest; he sits on the throne of the LORD, and all Israel obeys him. David dies in a good old age after forty years — seven in Hebron, thirty-three in Jerusalem — his acts written in the books of Samuel, Nathan, and Gad.",
    context:"Israel offers willingly for the temple, David's blessing of praise crowns his reign, and Solomon is enthroned as David dies full of days.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"1 Chronicles 29:11", text:"Thine, O LORD, is the greatness, and the power, and the glory, and the victory, and the majesty: for all that is in the heaven and in the earth is thine; thine is the kingdom, O LORD, and thou art exalted as head above all." },
      { ref:"1 Chronicles 29:14", text:"But who am I, and what is my people, that we should be able to offer so willingly after this sort? for all things come of thee, and of thine own have we given thee." },
      { ref:"1 Chronicles 29:23", text:"Then Solomon sat on the throne of the LORD as king instead of David his father, and prospered; and all Israel obeyed him." }
    ],
    takeaway:"All things come from God; whatever we give to His house is only returning what is already His.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"How much silver did the chief fathers and captains give for the house of God?", choices:["One thousand talents","Ten thousand talents","Seven thousand talents","Eighteen thousand talents"], a:1, why:"They gave for the service of the house of God ten thousand talents of silver, with five thousand talents of gold, brass, and iron." },
      { q:"What question did David put to the congregation?", choices:["Who will reign in my stead?","Who will carry the ark to Zion?","Who then is willing to consecrate his service this day unto the LORD?","Who will number Israel this day?"], a:2, why:"After describing his own giving, David asked who was willing to consecrate his service that day unto the LORD." },
      { q:"How long did David reign, and how was it divided?", choices:["Twenty years — ten in Hebron, ten in Jerusalem","Forty years — twenty in Hebron and twenty in Jerusalem","Sixty years — forty in Hebron and twenty in Jerusalem","Forty years — seven in Hebron and thirty and three in Jerusalem"], a:3, why:"David reigned forty years over Israel: seven years in Hebron and thirty and three years in Jerusalem, and died in a good old age." }
    ] }
);
